From 9a11487b6cc0ca20010b81e286da18a0fcd42bde Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 00:39:49 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] perf: IOP code a run recorded runs as translated blocks

The IOP interpreter took about 13% of the EE thread in a UN5 battle on the
Switch, nearly all of it in the sound modules (CRI ADX decoding about half,
Sony's modhsyn about a third), some 100k instructions a frame.

PS2_IOP_RECORD=<dir> writes, at the end of a run, the IOP's RAM, the words
it executed and the ones it entered other than by stepping on. The new
tool ioprecomp turns one or more such records into C++ blocks: from each
entry, the branch targets and the continuations of branches, straight on
over executed instructions to a branch and its delay slot (or up to 64
instructions), leaving system calls, COP0 and the runtime's HLE stubs to
the interpreter. Each block does what the interpreter does, one cycle per
instruction; an access outside RAM sets pc as the interpreter has it and
checks for a requested thread switch, as the interpreter does after every
instruction (nothing else can ask for one), and ends the block there.

The interpreter takes a block at its first instruction, outside a delay
slot, with budget for all of it, and once RAM has been found to hold the
words it was made from in the current code generation (a new one at each
module load, state load and IOP vblank). PS2_IOP_INTERPRET=1 turns them off.
The CPU struct and the block API moved to ps2/iop_cpu.hpp for the
generated code.

UN5 on the Mac, records of the four regression runs: 6470 blocks, 57615
instructions; 95% of IOP instructions run in blocks and the IOP's share of
CPU samples falls from 11.4% to 1.8%. Frames are identical and a state
saved at the end of the battle replay matches the interpreter's (apart
from the bytes that differ between two interpreter runs as well).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
